add(60,
'A revised order model can look cleaner while breaking previously valid clients. Review compatibility against accepted inputs and public outputs, not just new field names.',
'Compare a previous omittable note field with a revised required one. An old request omitting note now fails. Likewise, a tighter quantity constraint may reject a previously valid quantity. Keep representative old inputs and validate them against the revision. On the output side, compare generated paths and response schemas before approving removed or changed fields or operations.',
'A matching type name alone does not prove compatibility. Requiredness, constraints, fields and operations also shape client expectations.', 'LOW:data_table_demo')
add(61,
'A request wants a stored order, but a missing row is a valid lookup outcome to handle explicitly. Selection should match the intended identity or bounded condition.',
'For an identified order, await session.get(Order, identity) returns the entity or None. Trace both branches before using entity attributes. For a conditional selection, use session.scalars with select(Order).where(condition) and inspect the entity values. Add limit(n) when selecting a bounded number of rows. Compare one matching order, no matching orders and multiple matches under the bound. A result of None and an empty selection both represent absence in their respective lookup forms; neither should be treated as an existing entity. This exercise connects the lookup shape to the result handling rather than turning a selection into an unbounded application-wide search.',
'A successful lookup operation does not guarantee a row exists. Handle None or an empty result explicitly.')
add(62,
'Two related order writes must succeed or fail together. A transaction makes that joint boundary explicit rather than letting each write be treated as independently durable.',
'Place related writes inside async with session.begin(). Successful exit commits; exceptional exit rolls back. Trace a first write followed by a failing second write: the exceptional transaction outcome should discard uncommitted changes rather than report a completed pair. Compare that with successful exit, which makes the changes durable. If a flush fails, explicitly roll back before reusing the session, because its transactional state requires recovery. Follow both data state and session state in the trace: a caught error message alone does not establish that either boundary has been recovered. The grouping should come from the requirement that the writes succeed or fail together, not merely from their proximity in the source file.',
'Catching a flush exception does not restore the session to usable transactional state. Roll back before reuse.', 'HIGH:whiteboard_drawing')
add(63,
'A protected handler first needs to obtain a presented bearer credential. Extracting it is not the same as verifying that it is valid.',
'Declare Annotated[HTTPAuthorizationCredentials, Depends(HTTPBearer())] and read its scheme and credentials fields. A request with Authorization: Bearer followed by a supplied token presents the credential for later verification. With default auto_error=True, absent credentials and a non-bearer scheme are rejected instead of passed to the handler. Exercise absent and malformed Authorization headers separately so the extraction gate is visible. Then consider a presented bearer token: obtaining its text establishes only that extraction found a credential, not that its claims or authority are trustworthy. Keep that distinction in the trace so a handler does not mistake possession of a token-shaped value for completed authentication.',
'HTTPBearer extracts a credential; extraction does not verify claims or issue a token. Do not use the extracted text as proof of validity.')
add(64,
'Repeated reads of an order may reuse a cached copy, but the database remains authoritative. Cache-aside separates accelerated reads from source-of-truth storage.',
'Trace a read for order 42. Check its cache key first. On a miss, load from the primary store, cache the result and return it. A later read may reuse that copy if the staleness requirements permit it. Choose the key from the identity and parameters of the represented result: order 43 or a differently parameterized result must not accidentally reuse order 42\'s value. Compare a hit and a miss in a two-path diagram, keeping both connected to the same represented result. Cache-aside is useful for read-heavy reuse, not a claim that any data can remain cached indefinitely. The cache is a disposable copy; the primary store is still the authority when deciding what the stored order actually is.',
'A cache hit does not make the cache authoritative. A key must distinguish results whose identity or parameters differ.', 'HIGH:whiteboard_drawing')
add(65,
'A schema change needs a reviewed revision, not an assumption that automatic comparison understands every intended change.',
'Generate a candidate with alembic revision --autogenerate -m "description" using target_metadata. Inspect upgrade and downgrade, especially a drop-and-add pair that might actually represent a rename. Dropping a column deletes its data; a later apparent reversal may not recover it. Apply with alembic upgrade head and exercise the proposed downgrade on a test database. Candidate generation, review and reversal verification are separate steps.',
'Autogeneration is a candidate, not complete schema intent. A downgrade that recreates a column does not necessarily recover deleted data.', 'HIGH:screen_demo')
add(66,
'A frequent order lookup may benefit from an index, but adding one changes storage and write costs too. Choose an access path for a workload, not for every available column.',
'Imagine frequent equality lookups on an external reference. A PostgreSQL B-tree index is a candidate because it supports equality and ordered comparisons. The supplied declaration form is CREATE INDEX name ON table (column). Propose that index for the frequent lookup while explicitly accounting for storage and maintenance during writes. Compare the benefit to the query workload with the added overhead; the fact that a column exists is not sufficient justification. For an ordered lookup, B-tree support makes it another candidate, not a guarantee of a particular performance gain. State the workload and the cost trade-off together so the recommendation is bounded by what this index is meant to help.',
'An index can speed selected-row access but adds overhead. Do not turn that possibility into a guarantee or index every column automatically.', 'HIGH:screen_demo')
add(67,
'An order row and the JSON contract may describe the same entity while exposing different fields. Persistence and public API models have different responsibilities.',
'Keep persistence mapping separate from public input and output BaseModel classes. Put only intended output fields in PublicOutput and set response_model=PublicOutput. A storage-only processing field should not become public merely because it exists on the row object. If the public model should validate object attributes, set ConfigDict(from_attributes=True). That supports attribute-based input to the public model without making the persistence class the public schema.',
'The same entity does not require the same storage and public field set. Attribute-based validation is not permission to expose every attribute.', 'LOW:whiteboard_drawing')
add(68,
'A recognized uniqueness conflict should become the API\'s declared public outcome, not a leak of database exception text.',
'Register an exception handler for a recognized FailureClass at the API boundary. For a uniqueness conflict whose contract declares 409, translate the recognized failure to that outcome. If persistence has failed, roll back the transaction before further session use. Trace the failure through two responsibilities: recovery restores the persistence boundary, while translation expresses the public HTTP meaning. Merely translating the exception does not prove the session recovered, and merely rolling back does not specify what the client receives. Keep the public response intentional and avoid exposing database internals. The declared conflict mapping applies to a recognized case, not to every exception that happens during a write.',
'Do not map every database failure to conflict or treat a failed session as usable because an HTTP response was produced.', 'HIGH:whiteboard_drawing')
add(69,
'Clients need a stable error shape even when recognized exceptions arise from different internal code. The envelope is a public contract, not a dump of exception internals.',
'An exception handler receives request and exception and can return JSONResponse with the chosen status and public content. If FastAPI and Starlette HTTP exceptions should share this shape, register for Starlette\'s HTTPException. For a hypothetical error envelope containing a public reason, preserve the recognized status and relevant headers while changing the body format. Compare a recognized exception carrying required response metadata with one without it: formatting the body should not silently discard that metadata. Keep stack traces and raw exception details outside the envelope. This separates three concerns a client can observe: status, headers and body structure. A stable body alone is not enough if constructing it changes the underlying HTTP outcome.',
'Reformatting an error body should not discard the recognized status or relevant headers. Public structure is not raw internal exception detail.')
add(70,
'The API is reached through a public HTTPS proxy while its backend address is internal. Forwarded metadata is useful only when its sender belongs to the configured trust boundary.',
'Configure --forwarded-allow-ips with trusted proxy addresses. Allowing * trusts all senders and is appropriate only when network access is restricted to trusted proxies. If the proxy removes an external prefix such as /api, set root_path for that prefix. Verify redirects and documentation through the public HTTPS address, checking scheme and external prefix rather than inspecting only the backend URL. Trace public request, trusted proxy and backend as three distinct points. The proxy may report original scheme and client information, but a header carrying that information is not trustworthy merely because its name is familiar. The configured source boundary establishes whether the server should accept it.',
'Forwarded headers do not authenticate their own sender. Trust only configured proxy sources; wildcard trust needs restricted network access.', 'HIGH:whiteboard_drawing')
add(71,
'A webhook sender needs an acknowledgment of accepted delivery. That response does not necessarily mean every downstream business action has finished.',
'Declare the accepted media type, event shape and required fields for a hypothetical order event. Bind a typed model and set the operation\'s declared status_code. Verify accepted and invalid bodies against that acknowledgment contract. When deferred processing is permitted, return the declared 2xx promptly after acceptance and keep slow downstream work outside the acknowledgment path. Draw delivery validation, acknowledgment and downstream work separately rather than labeling all three "success." This makes the receiver\'s response meaningful without claiming completion it does not establish. The example is conditional on the integration allowing deferred work; do not invent that permission if the supplied provider contract does not grant it.',
'A successful delivery acknowledgment is not proof that all downstream business work completed.', 'HIGH:whiteboard_drawing')
add(72,
'One order request causes an outbound call. A nonsensitive interaction identifier links their records without relying on timestamps alone.',
'Bind the configured header with Header(alias=header_name). Keep or create a bounded nonsensitive identifier under service policy, pass the same identifier in the outbound headers, and include it in related local events. Verify the received header and logs. With concurrent requests, check that their contexts stay distinct rather than sharing one identifier accidentally.',
'A correlation identifier connects events; it is not proof of caller authority.', 'MEDIUM:whiteboard_drawing')
add(73,
'More errors in a busy interval do not automatically mean the service became less reliable. Separate traffic, failure fraction and latency before explaining a change.',
'Compare hypothetical equal windows: one has 100 requests and 5 failures, another 200 requests and 10 failures. Traffic and absolute failures doubled, but the failure fraction stayed at 5 percent. Request-counter rate measures events over time; compare failure rate divided by request rate over the same window and label grouping. Inspect histogram-derived latency independently to ask whether responses slowed. Partition by bounded categories such as method, route template and outcome, not raw caller identifiers. Now compare a delayed sample with a failing sample: longer waits and increased failures answer different questions. Match time windows and groups before drawing conclusions so changing traffic volume does not masquerade as a changed failure proportion.',
'Absolute error count is not failure fraction, and event frequency is not response duration.', 'HIGH:data_table_demo')
add(74,
'An orders listing should return a bounded window from a defined ordered result. Page boundaries need stable tie-breaking rather than unspecified order among equal sort values.',
'Use the same filter and ordering for page selections, with limit(page_size) and offset(offset). If several orders share the primary sort value, add a unique tie-breaker. In a hypothetical page size of three with offset three, OFFSET skips the first three result rows and LIMIT caps the next returned rows at three. Compare adjacent pages using the same predicate and unique ordering; changing the filter would change the result set being paged. Bound accepted page size and offset with numeric constraints and compute the offset according to the supplied page contract. This is bounded selection over an ordered result, not permission for a client to request an unlimited read.',
'LIMIT caps rows after OFFSET skips them. A repeated primary sort value needs a unique tie-breaker for deterministic ordering.', 'HIGH:data_table_demo')
add(75,
'A caller-supplied order reference is data, not SQL structure. Keep it bound as a value rather than concatenating it into executable query text.',
'Use a SQLAlchemy condition such as Entity.field == supplied_value in a select expression. For supplied textual SQL, use a :value placeholder and a parameter dictionary. Now compare a client-selected sort direction: a parameter cannot stand for arbitrary SQL structure. Map that structural choice through a defined allowlist. Separate value binding from structural selection so a safe mechanism for one is not assumed to solve the other.',
'Parameters bind data values, not arbitrary table names, columns or sort directions.', 'MEDIUM:annotated_code')
add(76,
'A newly constructed order object is not yet evidence of a committed database row. Creation moves through pending insertion, flush and commit.',
'Construct the entity and add it to the session. It is now pending insertion. Flush emits the insert and can assign database-generated identity values back when the mapping and database support it. Commit through the transaction contract to make successful creation durable. Then use an independent session to retrieve that identity and verify committed fields. Trace object construction, pending state, insert and durable lookup separately: an id observed after flush does not make flush a commit. The independent lookup verifies persistence rather than only re-reading the same constructed Python object. This gives the creation operation evidence at the stored-state boundary it claims to change.',
'Flush is not commit. A constructed object or assigned identity alone does not prove durable creation.')
add(77,
'Deleting an order should remove the intended persistent row, not just change an in-memory impression of it. Absence and transaction completion both matter.',
'Load the intended identity with session.get and handle None before attempting deletion. Await session.delete(entity) to mark the persistent entity for removal; flush emits the deletion. Commit successful removal under the contract, or roll back a failed deletion. Verify through a fresh lookup or independent session that the intended identity is absent and another row was not removed instead. Trace lookup, marking, flush and committed absence as distinct evidence. Reporting success before commitment would confuse a scheduled or uncommitted removal with a durable one. Choose two synthetic order identities for verification so the test checks both the removed target and the unaffected other row.',
'Marking an entity for deletion is not proof of durable absence. Verify the intended identity after successful commitment.')
add(78,
'A partial order update should change supplied permitted fields while retaining omitted values. Verify that rule in committed stored state, not just the request model.',
'Load the persistent order and extract supplied changes using model_dump(exclude_unset=True). Assign only permitted supplied fields. The session tracks attribute changes and emits UPDATE at flush. Commit on success and roll back failure before further session use. In an example supplying a new note but omitting quantity, reload in an independent session and compare both fields: note should reflect the committed change and quantity should retain its previous value. This two-part assertion demonstrates selective update more strongly than checking the new note alone. Trace intent extraction, assignment and committed reload separately so a default on an omitted field cannot silently become an update.',
'Inspecting only the request model does not verify persistence. Check changed supplied fields and unchanged omitted fields after commitment.')
add(79,
'A token can reveal claims when decoded without verification, but those claims remain untrusted. The verifier establishes a configured trust boundary before protected output is allowed.',
'Choose the allowed algorithm, key, issuer and audience from trusted configuration, not the token alone. Decode with verification and explicitly require exp, iss and aud. Requiring a claim and checking its value are distinct: a time check is not the same as guaranteeing the claim is present. Expiration and not-before constrain time validity. Trace a token through verification, then trusted context; a library verification exception should lead to credential rejection rather than fallback use of unverified claims. Compare a token merely opened for inspection with one accepted under all configured checks. The readable payload in the first case is information, not evidence that the caller is entitled to use its asserted identity or permissions.',
'Decoding without verification is not authentication. The token itself must not choose the allowed algorithm.', 'HIGH:annotated_code')
